Here is a simple Rust program which utilizes threads to find the smallest even number and the largest prime number from a given list:

```rust
use std::io;
use std::thread;
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};

fn is_prime(n: i32) -> bool {
    match n {
        0 | 1 => false,
        2 => true,
        _even if n % 2 == 0 => false,
        _ => {
            let sqrt_limit = (n as f64).sqrt() as i32;
            !(3..=sqrt_limit).step_by(2).any(|i| n % i == 0)
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let mut input = String::new();
    io::stdin().read_line(&mut input).expect("Failed to read line");
    let numbers: Vec<i32> = input.split_whitespace().map(|s| s.parse().unwrap()).collect();

    let smallest_even = Arc::new(Mutex::new(i32::MAX));
    let largest_prime = Arc::new(Mutex::new(i32::MIN));

    let smallest_even_clone = Arc::clone(&smallest_even);
    let thread_smallest = thread::spawn(move || {
        for &i in &numbers {
            if i % 2 == 0 {
                let mut smallest = smallest_even_clone.lock().unwrap();
                *smallest = i32::min(*smallest, i);
            }
        }
    });

    let largest_prime_clone = Arc::clone(&largest_prime);
    let thread_largest = thread::spawn(move || {
        for &i in &numbers {
            if is_prime(i) {
                let mut largest = largest_prime_clone.lock().unwrap();
                *largest = i32::max(*largest, i);
            }
        }
    });

    thread_smallest.join().expect("Smallest Even thread panicked");
    thread_largest.join().expect("Largest Prime thread panicked");

    println!("Smallest Even: {}", *smallest_even.lock().unwrap());
    println!("Largest Prime: {}", *largest_prime.lock().unwrap());
}
```

This code has two threads, one for finding the smallest even number and one for calculating the largest prime number. 

The smallest even number thread calculates the smallest even number by iterating over the list of numbers and if the number is even, it checks if it's less than the current smallest number.

The largest prime number thread calculates the largest prime number by iterating over the list of numbers and if the number is a prime, it checks if it's more than the current largest number.

Both smallest_even and largest_prime are shared between threads via Arc, and Mutex is used to ensure thread safety. 

Please note that this program assumes that the given list contains both at least one even number and at least one prime number; otherwise it will print the maximum and minimum values for an i32 type respectively. If you want to handle these error cases specifically, you would need to add logic to do so.

