&lt;p&gt;An undisclosed integer $t$ is randomly chosen within the confines of $1 \le t \le n$. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The objective is to ascertain the value of $t$ through a series of iterative guesses, represented by integer $g$. Following each guess, three potential outcomes may arise, revealing either $g \lt t$, $g = t$, or $g \gt t$. This procedure can be repeated as needed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Typically, the average number of guesses can be minimized using a binary search: With a lower limit $L$ and upper limit $H$ (initialized to $L = 1$ and $H = n$), let $g = \lfloor(L+H)/2\rfloor$ where $\lfloor \cdot \rfloor$ signifies the integer floor function. If $g = t$, the process concludes. Conversely, if $g \lt t$, set $L = g+1$, but if $g \gt t$, set $H = g - 1$. After establishing the new limits, the search process resumes, and ultimately concludes once $t$ is discovered. Even if $t$ can be inferred without searching, assume that a search will be necessary to verify the value.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your acquaintance Bob is of the opinion that the conventional binary search is not significantly superior to his randomized variant: Instead of setting $g = \lfloor(L+H)/2\rfloor$, simply allow $g$ to be a random integer within the range of $L$ and $H$, inclusive. The remainder of the algorithm mirrors the standard binary search. This modified search routine will be referred to as a &lt;dfn&gt;random binary search&lt;/dfn&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Given that $1 \le t \le n$ for random $t$, let $B(n)$ represent the expected number of guesses required to discover $t$ using the standard binary search, and let $R(n)$ represent the expected number of guesses required to discover $t$ using the random binary search. For instance, $B(6) = 2.33333333$ and $R(6) = 2.71666667$ when rounded to $8$ decimal places.&lt;/p&gt;

