```c++
#include<bits/stdc++.h>

using namespace std;

// A singly linked list node
struct Node {
    int data;
    Node* next;
};

// function to insert a new node at the beginning 
// of the linked list
Node* push(Node*& head, int data) {
    Node* node = new Node();
    node->data = data;
    node->next = head;
    head = node;
    return head;
}

int main() {
    Node *head = NULL;

    // initial linked list
    head = push(head, 10);
    head = push(head, 8);
    head = push(head, 7);

    int value = 5;

    // insert value in front of linked list
    head = push(head, value);

    // print updated linked list
    Node* temp = head;
    while (temp != NULL) {
        cout << temp->data << " ";
        temp = temp->next;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

The given initial linked list is [7, 8, 10], which is created in a reverse order, i.e., first 10 is pushed, then 8, and then 7. Then values 5 is pushed to the front of the list using the same push function. You should see output: `5 7 8 10 `, which verifies that the value was correctly inserted in front of the linked list. 

Remember to free any memory allocated with `new` to avoid memory leaks once the list is no longer needed (omitted for brevity in this example).

Here is a straightforward implementation of the Tower of Hanoi problem using recursion in C++. It prints the steps to be followed to move the disks from one tower to another.

```c++
#include <iostream> 

void TowerOfHanoi(int n, char from, char to, char aux) 
{ 
  // base case
  if (n == 1) 
  { 
    std::cout << "Move disk 1 from " << from << " to " <<  to << std::endl; 
    return; 
  } 

  // Step 1: Move n-1 disks from 'from' to auxiliary tower, Use 'to' as auxiliary tower
  TowerOfHanoi(n-1, from, aux, to); 

  // Step 2: Move the nth disk from 'from' to 'to'
  std::cout << "Move disk " << n << " from " << from << " to " <<  to << std::endl;

  // Step 3: Move the n-1 disks that we left on auxiliary tower to 'to'
  // Use 'from' as auxiliary tower
  TowerOfHanoi(n-1, aux, to, from); 
} 

int main() 
{
  int n = 4; // Number of disks
  TowerOfHanoi(n, 'A', 'C', 'B');  // A, B and C are names of towers
  return 0; 
} 
```
However, the problem with recursion is that the time complexity of this implementation is exponential. The time complexity is O(2^n), and considering 20 disks, the number of operations becomes huge and most likely will not run within 1 second. 

