answer:
Yes, you can retrieve this information using the Windows API. You can use it to access Terminal Services to get a list of active sessions. Unfortunately, it's not trivial because the API does not provide a direct way to get that list.

However, there's an indirect way:

1. Call `WTSEnumerateSessions` to get a list of currently active sessions. This will give you a list of `WTS_SESSION_INFO` structures.

2. For each session, call `WTSQuerySessionInformation` with `WTSUserName` and `WTSDomainName` to get the user name and domain name for the session.

This approach requires administrative privileges because the `WTSEnumerateSessions` function requires the `SeTcbPrivilege` privilege. If you run your application with normal user privileges, you must enable this privilege in your user account.

You can do the same using PowerShell. You can easily get logged in user details on a machine or on several machines in one go. Here's the simple command to check the logged in user:

```powershell
Get-WmiObject -Class Win32_ComputerSystem | Select-Object UserName
```

If you want this for multiple remote servers, you just need to pass the server list as input to this command. Replace "serverName" with your server's name.

```powershell
Get-WmiObject -ComputerName "serverName" -Class Win32_ComputerSystem | Select-Object UserName 
```

If you are making a program in .NET, you can use the `System.Management` namespace to run the WMI query. Here is a basic example:

```csharp
//add reference System.Management
using System.Management;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        ManagementScope ms = new ManagementScope("\\\\localhost");

        ObjectQuery query = new ObjectQuery("SELECT * FROM Win32_ComputerSystem");
        ManagementObjectSearcher searcher = new ManagementObjectSearcher(ms, query);

        ManagementObjectCollection collection = searcher.Get();
        foreach (ManagementObject mo in collection)
        {
            foreach (PropertyData property in mo.Properties)
            {
                if (property.Name == "UserName")
                {
                    Console.WriteLine("{0}", property.Value);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This will give you the username of the currently logged in user on the local machine. You can change the `"localhost"` string to the name of the remote computer you wish to query. Note that the username will be in DOMAIN/username format.

