Sure, I'd be happy to guide you through it. React Navigation is very comprehensive and can handle a lot of use cases tossed at it. The following steps outline the installation and basic usage of React Navigation in a React Native app:

1. Installation:
First, you need to install the necessary packages. In your project directory, you’ll need to install three packages:
   - **@react-navigation/native** : 
   It provides functionality for navigation, handling back actions or updating the current state.
   - **@react-navigation/stack** : 
   It provides a way for your app to transition between screens where each new screen is placed on top of a stack.
   - And you’ll also need to install **react-native-screens** and **react-native-safe-area-context**. 

```
npm install @react-navigation/native
npm install @react-navigation/stack
npm install react-native-screens react-native-safe-area-context
```
or if you use yarn:
```
yarn add @react-navigation/native
yarn add @react-navigation/stack
yarn add react-native-screens react-native-safe-area-context
```
These packages are the fundamental building blocks of Navigation in your app.

2. Initializing Navigation:
After installing the necessary packages, it's time to create our Navigator. Let's create two dummy screens for our app. Let's call them 'Home' and 'Details'. For creating a stack navigator, add the following 'App.js' file:

```jsx
import 'react-native-gesture-handler';
import * as React from 'react';
import { NavigationContainer } from '@react-navigation/native';
import { createStackNavigator } from '@react-navigation/stack';
import HomeScreen from './src/screens/HomeScreen';
import DetailScreen from './src/screens/DetailScreen';

const Stack = createStackNavigator();

function App() {
  return (
    <NavigationContainer>
      <Stack.Navigator>
        <Stack.Screen name="Home" component={HomeScreen} />
        <Stack.Screen name="Details" component={DetailScreen} />
      </Stack.Navigator>
    </NavigationContainer>
  );
}

export default App;
```
- Make sure to wrap all the screens within the NavigationContainer tag.
- Stack.Navigator component helps to transition between screens where each new screen is placed on top of the stack.
- 'name' prop is a unique identifier for this screen and it can be used to navigate between screens.
- 'component' prop is the component to display for this screen.

3. Configure Screens:
Now, within your HomeScreen.js and DetailScreen.js files, you may have something simple like this:

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import {View, Text, Button} from 'react-native';

function HomeScreen({ navigation }) {
  return (
    <View>
      <Text>Welcome to the Home Screen</Text>
      <Button
        title="Go to Details Screen"
        onPress={() => navigation.navigate('Details')}
      />
    </View>
  );
}

export default HomeScreen;
```
In this setup, 'Details' in navigation.navigate('Details') matches the 'name' prop that you provided to Stack.Screen in your App.js file. This connection enables navigation between your different screens.

4. Use navigationOptions for customization:
If you want to change the title, header styles or add buttons to the header then you can use navigationOptions. It's an object or a method that allows configuring the navbar, as well as the current screen inside your React Native application. Add navigationOptions either in navigationOptions prop in the screen component or in Stack.Screen in the stack navigator.

```jsx
<Stack.Screen 
  name="Home" 
  component={HomeScreen} 
  options={{ title: 'Home Page' }}
/>

_stack = createStackNavigator(
  {
    Screen_Name_1: 
    {
      screen: Screen_Name_1,
      navigationOptions: {
        title: 'Screen Title 1'
      },
    },
    Screen_Name_2: { 
      screen: Screen_Name_2,
      navigationOptions: {
        title: 'Screen Title 2'
      },
    },
  }
);
```

React Navigation is a complex subject with many other configuration options and features available. Given the complexity, it is beneficial to consult the official documentation (https://reactnavigation.org/docs/getting-started/) and understand the full capabilities of the library. 

