question:
I have this array a = ["one", "two"] and I want to match these values in dictionary I have which looks like this

b = {one: "something", two: "something2", three: "something3"}


I want to match this key and remove the keys from the dictionary
In the end it will be something like this

{'three': 'something3'}


what I have tried is this

for t in a:
    b = b.pop(t, None)
    return b


Just want to know if there is a better way to do this

answer:
You should be careful with your code because using pop method on the dictionary will not only remove it but also return the value of the key you popped which may cause undesirable results. Here's a safer way to do it:

```python
a = ["one", "two"]
b = {"one": "something", "two": "something2", "three": "something3"}

for key in a:
    if key in b:
        del b[key]
```

If you want to use one-liner Pythonic way using dictionary comprehension:

```python
b = {k: v for k, v in b.items() if k not in a}
```

Both ways, your result will be:

```python
{'three': 'something3'}
```

