Yes, it is possible to replace a standard component with a subclassed one at runtime, however, proper care should be taken when it comes to creating and releasing components.

The most suggested way is to free the component and replace it after the form is created completely (you could use `OnFormActivate` event for instance).

Here is an example of how to replace `TEdit` with a custom `TEdit` descendant (let's call it 'TMyEdit'):

```delphi
procedure TForm1.FormActivate(Sender: TObject);
var
  MyEdit: TMyEdit;
begin
  // Create an instance of TMyEdit
  MyEdit := TMyEdit.Create(Self);
  try
    // Assign properties from TEdit1 to MyEdit
    MyEdit.Name := 'MyEdit1';
    MyEdit.Text := Edit1.Text;
    MyEdit.Parent := Edit1.Parent;
    MyEdit.Align := Edit1.Align;
    MyEdit.BoundsRect := Edit1.BoundsRect;

    // Free TEdit1
    Edit1.Free;
    // Reassign the local variable Edit1 to MyEdit
    Edit1 := MyEdit;
  except
    // In case of any error free MyEdit
    MyEdit.Free;  
    raise;
  end;
end;
```
Please note:
- `Edit1` should be a public or published property, otherwise you can't access it outside the form;
- Make sure `Edit1` is not used elsewhere (for example, event handlers) before you replace it;
- In case of failed attempt to replace `Edit1`, an exception will be raised.


Freeing a component is a delicate action. Always ensure there are no references left to the component to be freed. Event handlers, properties in other components referring to the object, should all be cleared. If not, they will hold invalid pointers leading to arcane errors when they are accessed.

