You may not manually interact with the web page using C#. However, you can write a server-side code that makes a HTTP POST request to submit the data; an approach often referred to as web scraping. 

Here are the steps:

1. To send a POST request, you'll need to create a WebRequest instance, passing the Form's action URL endpoint to the Create method. 

2. Set the Method property to POST.

3. Prepare your post data and convert it to a byte array.

4. Set the ContentLength property to the length of your byte array, and then write the byte array to the request stream.

Here's a basic implementation:

```csharp
try
{
  string url = "http://www.stopforumspam.com/add";
  string postData = "ip=127.0.0.1&username=test&email=test%40test.com" // Replace these values with the actual values.

  // Convert our post data to byte array.
  byte[] data = Encoding.ASCII.GetBytes(postData);

  // Initiate new request.
  HttpWebRequest request = (HttpWebRequest)WebRequest.Create(url);

  // Set the ContentType to simulate form submission.
  request.ContentType = "application/x-www-form-urlencoded";
  request.ContentLength = data.Length;
  request.Method = "POST";

  // Write data to request stream.
  using (var stream = request.GetRequestStream())
  {
    stream.Write(data, 0, data.Length);
  }

  // Get response from the page.
  HttpWebResponse response = (HttpWebResponse)request.GetResponse();

  // Read the response to verify if our post was successful.
  string responseString = new StreamReader(response.GetResponseStream()).ReadToEnd();

  // Print the response.
  Console.WriteLine(responseString);
}
catch (WebException ex)
{
  // Handle exception here.
  Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
}
```

